.TH "shellkin\-stepdefs" "5" "March 2026" "shellkin\-stepdefs(5)" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
\f[B]shellkin\-stepdefs\f[R] \- step definitions file for shellkin
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shellkin step definitions are shell code files loaded from the
configured step definitions directory under the selected features root.
.PP
If present, \f[B]support.sh\f[R] in the features directory is sourced
before step definitions are loaded.
Additional support scripts can be loaded with repeatable
\f[B]\-\-load\f[R] entries or through the \f[B].shellkin\f[R] argfile.
Support script paths are relative to the selected features root.
.PP
Each step definition starts with a header line beginning with one of:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\(atGiven\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\(atWhen\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\(atThen\f[R]
.PP
The lines that follow are the body of the step definition and are
executed when the step matches.
.SH FORMAT
.SS Step Header
A step definition header declares the Gherkin step type and the pattern
to match.
.IP
.EX
\(atWhen I run \(aq{command}\(aq
.EE
.SS Step Body
The step body is plain shell code.
.IP
.EX
\(atWhen I run \(aq{command}\(aq
  run \(dq$command\(dq
.EE
.PP
Indenting the body is recommended for readability, but optional.
.PP
Each definition continues until the next valid step or hook header or
the end of the file.
.SS Tokens
Patterns may contain named tokens in braces.
.IP
.EX
\(atThen the file \(aq{path}\(aq should exist
  \f[B][[\f[R] \-f \(dq$path\(dq \f[B]]]\f[R]
.EE
.PP
When the step matches, each token is exported as a shell variable for
use in the body.
.PP
Token names must start with a letter or underscore, and may contain
letters, numbers, and underscores.
.PP
Quoted tokens accept either quote delimiter when the step runs.
For example, this definition:
.IP
.EX
\(atThen the text should include \(aq{text}\(aq
.EE
.PP
matches both of these steps, and captures the text without the outer
quotes:
.IP
.EX
Then the text should include \(dqSomething\(aqs wrong\(dq
Then the text should include \(aqJim \(dqJimbo\(dq Jackson\(aq
.EE
.PP
The opening and closing quote in the feature step must match.
Quoted token patterns do not match unquoted values.
.SH HOOKS
Step definition files can also declare scenario hooks with
\f[B]\(atBefore\f[R] and \f[B]\(atAfter\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\(atBefore
  mkdir \-p tmp

\(atAfter
  rm \-rf tmp

\(atBefore \(atneeds\-server
  ./server start

\(atAfter \(atneeds\-server
  ./server stop
.EE
.PP
Hooks without a tag run for every scenario.
Tagged hooks run only for scenarios with that tag, including tags
inherited from the feature.
.PP
\f[B]\(atBefore\f[R] hooks run before background and scenario steps.
If a \f[B]\(atBefore\f[R] hook fails, the scenario fails and the
remaining steps are skipped.
.PP
\f[B]\(atAfter\f[R] hooks run after scenario steps, even when a step or
\f[B]\(atBefore\f[R] hook fails.
If an \f[B]\(atAfter\f[R] hook fails, the scenario fails.
.PP
Passing hooks are quiet.
Failing hooks are shown in the error report.
.PP
Hooks can call helper functions from \f[B]support.sh\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# features/support.sh\f[R]
start_server() \f[B]{\f[R]
  ./server start
\f[B]}\f[R]

stop_server() \f[B]{\f[R]
  ./server stop
\f[B]}\f[R]
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# features/step_definitions/hooks.sh\f[R]
\(atBefore \(atneeds\-server
  start_server

\(atAfter \(atneeds\-server
  stop_server
.EE
.SH HELPERS
.SS run
Run a shell command and capture its result for later assertions.
.IP
.EX
\(atWhen I run \(aq{command}\(aq
  run \(dq$command\(dq
.EE
.PP
The \f[B]run\f[R] helper always returns success, even when the command
fails.
.SS fail
Fail the current step with an optional custom message.
.IP
.EX
\(atThen the output should include \(aq{text}\(aq
  \f[B][[\f[R] \(dq$LAST_STDOUT\(dq == *\(dq$text\(dq* \f[B]]]\f[R] \f[B]||\f[R] fail \(dqinvalid output detected\(dq
.EE
.SS defer
Register cleanup code to run when the current scenario finishes.
.IP
.EX
\(atGiven I am in a temp directory
  old_pwd=$PWD
  temp_dir=$(mktemp \-d)
  cd \(dq$temp_dir\(dq
  defer cd \(dq$old_pwd\(dq
  defer rm \-rf \(dq$temp_dir\(dq
.EE
.PP
Deferred actions are scoped to the current scenario.
They run after both passing and failing scenarios, and execute in
reverse order of registration.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Shellkin exposes these variables to step definition bodies:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]FAIL_MESSAGE\f[R] \- message supplied by the most recent
\f[B]fail\f[R] call
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]LAST_EXIT_CODE\f[R] \- exit status captured by the most recent
\f[B]run\f[R] call
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]LAST_STDOUT\f[R] \- standard output captured by the most recent
\f[B]run\f[R] call
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]LAST_STDERR\f[R] \- standard error captured by the most recent
\f[B]run\f[R] call
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]DOC_STRING\f[R] \- doc string attached to the current step, if any
.SH DOC STRINGS
If a feature step is followed by a Gherkin doc string, its content is
exposed to the step body through \f[B]DOC_STRING\f[R].
.IP
.EX
Then the output should match
  \(dq\(dq\(dq
  hello
  world
  \(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.IP
.EX
\(atThen the output should match
  \f[B][[\f[R] \(dq$LAST_STDOUT\(dq == \(dq$DOC_STRING\(dq \f[B]]]\f[R]
.EE
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# features/support.sh\f[R]
temp_workspace() \f[B]{\f[R]
  temp_dir=$(mktemp \-d)
  cd \(dq$temp_dir\(dq
\f[B]}\f[R]
.EE
.IP
.EX
\(atGiven I am in a temp directory
  old_pwd=$PWD
  temp_workspace
  defer cd \(dq$old_pwd\(dq
  defer rm \-rf \(dq$temp_dir\(dq

\(atWhen I run \(aq{command}\(aq
  run \(dq$command\(dq

\(atThen the file \(aq{path}\(aq should exist
  \f[B][[\f[R] \-f \(dq$path\(dq \f[B]]]\f[R]
.EE
